::CW_U03_UA_Q01::
[MLA.CW.FIC.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q01
Passage: A character gives up a winning speech slot to help a nervous friend prepare. What universal theme is best supported?{
~A. Speeches are always short.#Incorrect. Length is not the focus. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Friendship prevents all ambition.#Incorrect. The theme is balance, not removing ambition.
~C. Winning never matters.#Incorrect. The slot matters because giving it up is a sacrifice.
=D. Personal goals can still leave room for helping others.#Correct. The character sacrifices attention to support a friend.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q02::
[MLA.CW.FIC.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q02
Source element: A guide gives an object before a journey. Adaptation: A coach gives a route card before a city race. What is preserved?{
~A. The exact original setting.#Incorrect. The setting changes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A guide supports a journey with an object.#Correct. The coach and route card preserve the guide-object pattern.
~C. A copied plot.#Incorrect. The scene is transformed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. No source connection.#Incorrect. The source pattern remains. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q03::
[MLA.CW.FIC.06] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q03
Central idea: Avoiding responsibility can make guilt grow. Which detail best supports it?{
~A. The wall is painted white.#Incorrect. This is unrelated by itself. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A dog walks outside.#Incorrect. This does not connect to guilt. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The character hides the broken key after blaming someone else.#Correct. Hiding evidence after blame shows guilt. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The character eats breakfast.#Incorrect. This does not support responsibility. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q04::
[MLA.CW.FIC.07] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q04
Passage: "Naturally, the committee solved the leak by buying a larger bucket." What rhetorical choice is strongest?{
=A. Sarcasm criticizing an ineffective solution.#Correct. The sentence mocks the committee's poor response.
~B. Neutral admiration.#Incorrect. The tone is critical. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A scientific report.#Incorrect. The passage is rhetorical. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A theme comparison.#Incorrect. The question asks rhetoric. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q05::
[MLA.CW.WR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q05
Passage: Eli thinks family stories delay chores until he finds the same street names in his grandmother's old notebook. What changes?{
~A. Eli proves chores are impossible.#Incorrect. Chores are not the main realization. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Eli recognizes that the stories carry family history.#Correct. The notebook changes his perspective. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Eli stops listening forever.#Incorrect. The evidence suggests he values the stories.
~D. The notebook has no role.#Incorrect. It provides evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q06::
[MLA.CW.WR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q06
Passage A: Lina practices alone after failure. Passage B: Theo asks for help after failure. What comparison is strongest?{
~A. The passages are identical because both have names.#Incorrect. Names are surface details. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Only Passage A has a theme.#Incorrect. Both have themes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Failure ends growth.#Incorrect. Both continue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Both show growth after failure, but one emphasizes independent persistence while the other emphasizes support.#Correct. This compares shared theme and different development.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q07::
[MLA.CW.WR.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q07
Passage: Talia thinks asking for critique proves weakness, but later asks what to revise first. What coming-of-age change is shown?{
~A. She refuses all revision.#Incorrect. She asks what to revise. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. She stops making art.#Incorrect. No evidence says that. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. She learns feedback can be part of growth.#Correct. Her belief changes. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. She avoids the teacher.#Incorrect. She asks the teacher. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q08::
[MLA.CW.FIC.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q08
What is a complete theme statement?{
=A. Friendship can require honesty even when honesty is uncomfortable.#Correct. This is a complete idea. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Friendship.#Incorrect. This is only a topic. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The story has a friend.#Incorrect. This is summary. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Blue jacket.#Incorrect. This is a detail. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q09::
[MLA.CW.FIC.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q09
A student adapts a source by changing only character names while keeping the exact plot and ending. What is the problem?{
~A. It changes too much.#Incorrect. It changes very little. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. It is too close to copying and lacks meaningful transformation.#Correct. Adaptation requires purposeful change. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. It has no source connection.#Incorrect. It is overly close to the source.
~D. It is automatically respectful.#Incorrect. Respect also requires careful representation and purpose.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q10::
[MLA.CW.FIC.06] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q10
Passage: The clock ticks louder each time Ren avoids the question. What is the reader effect?{
~A. The clock explains math.#Incorrect. No math is involved. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The question disappears.#Incorrect. The question remains central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The sound detail increases tension around avoidance.#Correct. The ticking emphasizes delay. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The scene becomes cheerful.#Incorrect. The mood is tense. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q11::
[MLA.CW.FIC.07] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q11
Central idea: Memory can make a new place feel less lonely. Which revision best supports it?{
~A. Add unrelated traffic.#Incorrect. This distracts. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove every memory detail.#Incorrect. That weakens the idea. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. List random furniture.#Incorrect. Random detail does not support the idea.
=D. Change "blue rug" to "a blue rug the same color as Grandma's kitchen rug."#Correct. The revised detail connects memory to place.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q12::
[MLA.CW.WR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q12
Passage: A narrator repeats "not yet" while postponing the truth. What author purpose is likely?{
~A. To remove reader effect.#Incorrect. Repetition creates effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. To emphasize avoidance through repetition.#Correct. The repeated phrase mirrors delay. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. To explain cooking.#Incorrect. No cooking appears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. To prove the truth was told early.#Incorrect. It is postponed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q13::
[MLA.CW.WR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q13
Which detail best supports "courage can exist beside fear"?{
=A. Kai steps forward while hiding shaking hands.#Correct. The action shows fear and courage together.
~B. Kai feels no fear at all.#Incorrect. That removes the contrast. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Kai never moves.#Incorrect. Courage requires action here. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Kai changes socks.#Incorrect. Unrelated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q14::
[MLA.CW.WR.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q14
Source element: A forbidden door tests curiosity. Adaptation: A student is warned not to open a locked digital folder. What is preserved?{
~A. The exact physical door.#Incorrect. It becomes a digital folder. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. No conflict.#Incorrect. The warning creates conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The forbidden-choice test.#Correct. The door changes form, but the test remains.
~D. The same time period.#Incorrect. It is modernized. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q15::
[MLA.CW.FIC.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q15
Passage: A character returns a trophy after learning the score was wrong. What theme is strongest?{
~A. Trophies are always made of gold.#Incorrect. Material is not mentioned. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Scores never matter.#Incorrect. The score matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Honesty removes all conflict.#Incorrect. Honesty creates a difficult choice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Doing what is right can matter more than public praise.#Correct. The character gives up an undeserved reward.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q16::
[MLA.CW.FIC.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q16
Which explanation best connects evidence to central idea? Central idea: Secrets damage trust. Detail: The character deletes a message before a friend sees it.{
~A. It supports the idea because phones exist.#Incorrect. The phone alone is not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The deleted message supports the idea because hiding communication creates secrecy between friends.#Correct. This explains the connection. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. It supports the idea because it is a sentence.#Incorrect. That explains nothing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Details do not need connection.#Incorrect. Support requires connection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q17::
[MLA.CW.FIC.06] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q17
Passage: "Of course the award went to the student who arrived late and bowed like royalty." What tone is created?{
=A. Sarcastic and critical.#Correct. The wording criticizes the award choice. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Completely neutral.#Incorrect. The sentence has attitude. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Scientific.#Incorrect. It is not scientific. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Grateful.#Incorrect. It does not sound grateful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q18::
[MLA.CW.FIC.07] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q18
What should a rationale for adaptation include?{
~A. Only a new name.#Incorrect. A name is not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. No explanation.#Incorrect. A rationale explains. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Source element, transformation, and reason for the change.#Correct. These explain the adaptation. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Only punctuation.#Incorrect. Adaptation is about craft choices. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q19::
[MLA.CW.WR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q19
Passage: A character thinks leadership means doing everything alone, then succeeds only after trusting the team. What central idea is best supported?{
~A. Teams always fail.#Incorrect. The team helps. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Leaders should never trust anyone.#Incorrect. The passage shows the opposite. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Responsibility is unrelated.#Incorrect. It is central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Leadership can require shared responsibility.#Correct. The character changes through teamwork. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q20::
[MLA.CW.WR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q20
Passage: A narrator leaves home but packs a recipe card first. What theme is best supported?{
~A. Recipes erase memory.#Incorrect. The card preserves memory. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Change can include carrying meaningful parts of the past forward.#Correct. The card preserves memory during change. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Moving means forgetting everything.#Incorrect. The narrator carries memory. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Home is never important.#Incorrect. The card shows home matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q21::
[MLA.CW.WR.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q21
What is a blind spot in coming-of-age fiction?{
~A. A required eye injury.#Incorrect. It is about understanding. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A random setting detail.#Incorrect. It is a perspective limit. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Something a character does not yet understand.#Correct. Growth often begins when the blind spot is challenged.
~D. A complete theme statement.#Incorrect. It is not a theme statement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q22::
[MLA.CW.FIC.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q22
Passage: The writer gives the antagonist a quiet moment helping a child. What purpose might this serve?{
~A. To remove all conflict.#Incorrect. Complexity can deepen conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. To prove the character has no role.#Incorrect. It deepens the role. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. To change the passage into a recipe.#Incorrect. No recipe appears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. To complicate the reader's view of the character.#Correct. A humane action adds complexity. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q23::
[MLA.CW.FIC.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q23
Which comparison is strongest? Passage A: a child returns a stolen coin. Passage B: a merchant corrects an overpayment.{
=A. Both develop integrity through returning what does not belong to the character.#Correct. Both actions restore fairness. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Coins and merchants are identical.#Incorrect. They are different details. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Only children can be honest.#Incorrect. The merchant is honest too. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Money removes theme.#Incorrect. Money creates the integrity conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q24::
[MLA.CW.FIC.06] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q24
Scene plan: central idea about belonging, conflict about exclusion, and detail of a shared table. What makes it integrated?{
~A. It uses unrelated details.#Incorrect. Integration requires connection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The conflict and table detail both support the belonging idea.#Correct. The pieces point to the same meaning.
~C. The table has no role.#Incorrect. It can symbolize inclusion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The central idea should be removed.#Incorrect. It guides the scene. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q25::
[MLA.CW.FIC.07] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q25
A student writes, "The theme is that Kai finds a coin." What feedback is best?{
=A. Revise because that is a plot detail; explain the larger idea the coin supports.#Correct. Theme must be a complete idea. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Keep it because all plot details are themes.#Incorrect. Details support themes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Use one word only.#Incorrect. One-word topics are not complete themes.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q26::
[MLA.CW.WR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q26
Passage: Lena wanted to ignore the new student because everyone else did. At lunch, she moved her tray to the empty seat beside him. Which theme statement is strongest?{
~A. Lunch can happen at school.#Incorrect. This is a fact from the situation, not a theme.
~B. The topic is students.#Incorrect. "Students" is a topic, not a complete theme statement.
~C. Lena has a tray.#Incorrect. This only names a detail and does not explain the larger meaning.
=D. Belonging often begins when one person chooses kindness over comfort.#Correct. This is a complete universal idea supported by Lena's choice.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q27::
[MLA.CW.WR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q27
Passage: Tomas wanted to win the art prize, but he helped his tired partner finish her mural before starting his own final details. What topic and theme are best supported?{
~A. Topic: paint; Theme: Paint is colorful.#Incorrect. The passage is not mainly about paint color.
~B. Topic: sleep; Theme: Everyone should nap.#Incorrect. Tiredness is a detail, not the central theme.
=C. Topic: ambition and teamwork; Theme: Personal goals can still leave room for helping others.#Correct. Tomas wants to win, yet he chooses to help his partner.
~D. Topic: prizes; Theme: Prizes always ruin friendships.#Incorrect. The passage does not show prizes always causing harm.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q28::
[MLA.CW.WR.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q28
Passage: Asha kept the cracked bracelet after her friend moved away. She wore it on the first day at a new school, touching it before she introduced herself. What does the bracelet help develop?{
~A. A theme about jewelry prices.#Incorrect. The passage never focuses on cost. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A theme about memory giving strength during change.#Correct. The bracelet connects friendship, memory, and Asha's courage in a new place.
~C. A rule that all bracelets are cracked.#Incorrect. That is not a life idea or theme.
~D. A summary that Asha has a bracelet.#Incorrect. A summary is not a theme explanation.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q29::
[MLA.CW.FIC.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q29
Passage A: Rina gave her last pencil to a classmate before the exam. Passage B: Omar shared his umbrella even though his own sleeve got soaked. What shared theme is best supported?{
~A. School supplies and umbrellas are the same object.#Incorrect. The objects differ; the theme connection is generosity.
~B. Weather is always more important than school.#Incorrect. Only Passage B includes weather. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Exams should happen in the rain.#Incorrect. This combines details in a way the passages do not support.
=D. Helping others can require giving up personal comfort or convenience.#Correct. Both characters sacrifice something to help another person.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q30::
[MLA.CW.FIC.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q30
Passage A: Mina apologized after blaming her brother for the spill. Passage B: Carter returned the borrowed book after pretending he had lost it. How are the themes similar?{
=A. Both show that honesty can repair damage after a mistake.#Correct. Both characters admit or correct a dishonest action.
~B. Both are mainly about cleaning.#Incorrect. Only Passage A includes a spill, and cleaning is not the theme.
~C. Both show that books are dangerous.#Incorrect. The book is part of one conflict, not the shared theme.
~D. Both prove apologies are useless.#Incorrect. The passages show movement toward repair. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q31::
[MLA.CW.FIC.06] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q31
Source element: A character receives a warning before making a risky choice. Adaptation: A student ignores a teammate's warning about faulty robot code before the final round. What is preserved?{
~A. The exact original setting.#Incorrect. The setting has been transformed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The character names from the source.#Incorrect. No original names are being preserved. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The warning before a risky choice.#Correct. The adaptation keeps the warning-and-choice pattern.
~D. The complete original plot.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the plot situation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q32::
[MLA.CW.FIC.07] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.04
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q32
Source element: A guide gives a traveler a protective object. Adaptation: A grandmother gives a narrator a recipe card before the narrator moves to a new city. What is transformed?{
~A. The source is copied exactly.#Incorrect. The object and situation are changed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The protective object becomes emotional support through a recipe card.#Correct. The source object changes into a realistic family object with emotional meaning.
~C. The adaptation removes all source connection.#Incorrect. A guide gives an object before a difficult journey.
~D. The character has no journey.#Incorrect. Moving to a new city is a kind of journey.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q33::
[MLA.CW.WR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q33
Central idea: Avoiding responsibility can make guilt grow. Passage detail: Niko hides the baseball under three blankets after blaming the broken window on the wind. How does the detail support the central idea?{
~A. It shows Niko likes blankets.#Incorrect. The blankets matter because he uses them to hide evidence.
~B. It proves the wind broke the window.#Incorrect. The hidden baseball suggests responsibility. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It distracts from the central idea completely.#Incorrect. The detail directly supports the idea. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. It shows Niko's guilt growing through secretive action.#Correct. The hiding shows avoidance becoming more intense.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q34::
[MLA.CW.WR.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q34
Central idea: Accepting help can be a form of strength. Which detail best supports the idea?{
~A. The character describes an unrelated poster for three paragraphs.#Incorrect. This does not connect to accepting help.
~B. The character refuses all help and leaves.#Incorrect. This works against the central idea. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The character hands one heavy box to a friend and keeps walking beside her.#Correct. The action shows accepting help without giving up.
~D. The character counts clouds.#Incorrect. This is unrelated unless tied to the conflict.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q35::
[MLA.CW.WR.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q35
Passage: The writer repeats the image of an empty chair at dinner before explaining who is missing. What is the most likely purpose?{
=A. To make the reader feel absence before receiving full explanation.#Correct. Repetition builds mood and curiosity. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. To describe furniture only.#Incorrect. The repeated chair creates meaning beyond furniture.
~C. To prove the chair is new.#Incorrect. Age or condition is not the focus.
~D. To avoid all emotion.#Incorrect. The image creates emotion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q36::
[MLA.CW.FIC.02] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q36
Passage: "Of course the award went to the student who forgot the speech and bowed like royalty." What craft choice is strongest?{
~A. Warm admiration.#Incorrect. The wording sounds critical, not admiring. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Sarcastic tone.#Correct. "Of course" and "bowed like royalty" create criticism.
~C. Neutral listing.#Incorrect. The sentence has attitude. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Scientific explanation.#Incorrect. This is rhetorical tone, not science. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q37::
[MLA.CW.FIC.04] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q37
Passage: Talia thinks asking for critique proves she is not ready for advanced art. After her teacher explains that artists revise through feedback, Talia asks what to change first. What is Talia's changed understanding?{
~A. Artists never revise.#Incorrect. The teacher says artists revise. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Asking questions proves failure.#Incorrect. The passage shows the opposite. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Feedback can be part of serious growth.#Correct. Talia learns that asking for help supports improvement.
~D. Advanced art has no challenge.#Incorrect. The class is challenging. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q38::
[MLA.CW.FIC.06] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.03
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q38
Passage: Marco thinks his father's traffic rules are about control. After his sister almost rides into the street, he understands why his father checks twice. What causes Marco's change?{
~A. He forgets the rule.#Incorrect. He understands it better. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. His father disappears.#Incorrect. The father remains part of the conflict.
~C. The street becomes irrelevant.#Incorrect. The street creates the danger. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. He sees the safety reason behind the rule.#Correct. The near-accident changes his perspective. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q39::
[MLA.CW.FIC.07] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q39
Scene plan: Central idea: maturity includes accepting guidance. Source influence: a guide gives an object before a journey. Character conflict: Maya thinks leaders work alone, while her teammate thinks leaders trust the group. Which choice best explains integration?{
=A. The plan is integrated because every element supports shared responsibility.#Correct. The central idea, guide-object pattern, and perspective conflict all point to the same meaning.
~B. The plan is integrated because it has many unrelated parts.#Incorrect. Integration requires connection, not just many parts.
~C. The source influence distracts because objects can never develop theme.#Incorrect. Objects can support theme when connected to conflict and growth.
~D. The conflict should be removed so the theme is easier.#Incorrect. Conflict helps develop the theme. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_UA_Q40::
[MLA.CW.WR.01] MLA Standard: MLA.CW.FIC.02
Question ID: CW_U03_UA_Q40
Passage: Jalen believed the mural had to be his design alone. When Amara handed him a sketch showing how everyone's ideas could fit, he erased his signature from the center and redrew the wall as a shared map. What changed understanding is shown?{
~A. Jalen learns maps are never useful.#Incorrect. The shared map becomes meaningful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Jalen learns that leadership can include shared creative ownership.#Correct. He moves from control to collaboration. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Jalen decides art has no theme.#Incorrect. The mural develops theme. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Jalen avoids all collaboration.#Incorrect. He accepts Amara's input. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
